reasons={
 'USD':'Retained Slight weakening. Cheaper energy and renewed stress centred on US technology marginally reduce the defensive/inflation premium; the cash-driven USD 10 bn borrowing reduction is only a small reinforcement. The strongest opposite case is resilient private demand, persistent core PCE, hawkish colleagues and a two-year indication back near 4.61%, slightly above the prior session. That rebound defeats a continuing-front-end-decline explanation but does not remove the US-origin asset and energy channels. A sustained restoration of the expected Fed path or dollar funding stress would reverse the balance. CAD has clearer negative export-income pressure; AUD/NZD have weaker domestic activity but distinct policy and China offsets.',
 'EUR':'Retained Moderate strengthening. Lower oil and gas relieve an energy importer\u2019s real-income and financing burden across the session boundary, reinforced by resilient final manufacturing. Weak German retail, French fiscal risk, restrictive credit and less prospective ECB restraint are the strongest opposite case; the relief acts directly on those costs now and still leads. Renewed energy escalation or disorderly French financing invalidates it. EUR has a clearer current positive mechanism than GBP\u2019s already crowded tightening premium, while JPY/CHF defensive support is more dependent on renewed technology stress. Against CAD, the same energy shock has opposite income effects, supporting a Moderate EURCAD BUY through the European-opening checkpoint.',
 'GBP':'Retained Slight strengthening. Cheaper energy, resilient manufacturing and budget continuity modestly help spending power and financing. The strongest opposite case is an overextended expected BoE hiking path, soft household credit and labour slack: an expected Thursday hike is baseline and lower energy can reduce later restraint. Current income relief narrowly leads, but this is weaker than EUR\u2019s broader importer mechanism. A delivered patient BoE path, renewed energy stress or fiscal-financing deterioration defeats the advantage. Swiss defensive demand and factory resilience make a GBPCHF recovery insufficiently distinct to renew the old pair call before CPI.',
 'JPY':'Reversed Slight weakening to Slight strengthening for the new Asian session. The early US technology rebound failed by the close; renewed defensive incentives now combine with cheaper energy imports and the completed BoJ normalisation outlook. The strongest opposite case is broad non-tech equity resilience, Chinese support tools, a firm US two-year yield and Japan\u2019s funding-rate disadvantage. Those offsets limit the advantage, but the old premise of easing technology stress no longer dominates after the close. A durable regional risk recovery or a renewed foreign-rate advantage defeats this view. No new BoJ hike, December promise or measured private flow is asserted. CHF has firmer factory momentum; JPY has more normalisation support, leaving no compelling fresh JPYCHF preference.',
 'CHF':'Retained Slight strengthening. Renewed US technology stress and European fiscal concerns preserve modest defensive support, with strong Swiss manufacturing providing a domestic offset to softer retail and KOF momentum. The strongest opposite case is the zero-rate funding incentive and reduced geopolitical premium as diplomacy progresses. The mixed close restores enough protection demand for a limited positive balance; there is no new SNB tightening surprise. A soft underlying CPI package or durable broad risk recovery defeats it. EUR has the clearer cost-relief mechanism; JPY has more normalisation support, so equal Slight grades do not create a CHFJPY recommendation.',
 'CAD':'Retained Moderate weakening into the European energy handover. Oil relief has persisted beyond the US close, reducing Canada\u2019s energy-income support while manufacturing orders contract and recent GDP is weak. Constructive trade comments, narrower US-Canada yields and conditional BoC tightening are the strongest opposite case; none establishes delivered tariff relief or neutralises the immediate terms-of-trade loss. A confirmed trade agreement, sustained oil recovery or materially firmer BoC path defeats the judgment. This is a clearer negative mechanism than the slight AUD/NZD risk-demand balance and supports Moderate EURCAD BUY, bounded by the earlier shared European-opening checkpoint.',
 'AUD':'Retained Slight weakening before the RBA. Renewed technology stress, weak factory orders and the official China contraction marginally constrain external demand and energy receipts. The strongest opposite case is Bullock\u2019s domestic-capacity warning, persistent underlying inflation, the private China PMI beat, fresh Chinese equity support and Apple\u2019s resilient actual results. A likely 4.85% decision is the expected baseline; it does not alone establish a stronger continuing path, while equity liquidity does not establish commodity consumption. Those substantial offsets prevent Moderate or Strong negativity. A firmer multi-meeting RBA path or sustained trading-partner demand/risk recovery defeats weakness. AUD\u2019s capacity case is firmer than NZD\u2019s, but its global-tech exposure prevents forcing an AUDNZD recommendation.',
 'NZD':'Retained Slight weakening. September consents fell 7.9% and the RBNZ majority\u2019s weak household-demand and wage-transmission rationale still dominates over the regional handover. Strong annual consent growth, export/business pricing resilience, high conditional December hike likelihood, cheaper energy and Chinese support form the strongest opposite case. They make the negative advantage limited rather than a new collapse: stronger majority guidance or a broad regional-demand recovery would defeat it. The new consents actual is separate from tomorrow\u2019s still-unknown jobs and wages. AUD has firmer capacity support, but its technology exposure leaves the equal-grade cross insufficiently clear before RBA.'}
